    <h3>Legal Aspects: Is It Legal to Unlock Your Own PDF Files?</h3>
    <p>
      A frequent concern among users is whether decrypting password-protected PDF files is legally compliant under Indian law. The legal standing is unequivocal: <strong>it is completely legal to unlock documents of which you are the lawful owner or authorized custodian</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Under the <strong>Information Technology Act, 2000 (Section 43 and Section 66)</strong>, unauthorized access to computer systems and electronic records is an offense only when executed without the consent of the owner or person in charge. When a bank, insurance firm, or government agency issues an electronic statement to your verified email or provides a download through your authenticated NetBanking portal, you are the lawful owner and recipient of that electronic record.
    </p>
    <p>
      Banks apply passwords solely to fulfill regulatory compliance under Section 43A of the IT Act regarding <em>"Reasonable Security Practices and Procedures"</em> for data in transit over unsecured electronic mail protocols (SMTP). Once the document has arrived in your possession, stripping the password for archiving, printing, annotating, merging, or sharing with your legal counsel or tax accountant is considered lawful fair dealing.
    </p>
    <div class="unlock-callout" style="border-left-color: #3b82f6;">
      <h4 style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink);">Ethical & Copyright Boundaries</h4>
      <p>
        While unlocking your personal financial records, salary slips, and government IDs is 100% legal, it is strictly prohibited and illegal to use password removal tools to bypass <strong>Digital Rights Management (DRM)</strong> on copyrighted intellectual property—such as commercial eBooks, proprietary research publications, or corporate trade secrets—without explicit authorization from the copyright holder.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Under the Hood: Why Client-Side WebAssembly Decryption is Essential for Privacy</h3>
    <p>
      Traditional online PDF unlockers operate on an outdated client-server architecture: you upload your encrypted file to their web server, type your password into an online form, their server decrypts the file on their backend, and they send you a download link. This model exposes your most private data to catastrophic privacy risks:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Exposure of Sensitive Financial Footprints:</strong> Bank statements contain your home address, account balance, IFSC codes, monthly income, mutual fund investments, and UPI transaction recipients. Uploading these documents to foreign cloud servers creates an unnecessary data trail that could be leaked, cached, or scraped.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Server Logging of Passwords:</strong> On server-based sites, your personal password (often containing your PAN, mobile number, or date of birth) is sent over HTTP to a third-party server, where it can be stored in cleartext application server logs.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Our tool eliminates these vulnerabilities entirely using <strong>WebAssembly (Wasm)</strong>. When you access our unlock tool, your web browser downloads a compiled binary of <strong>QPDF</strong>—the open-source, industry-standard C++ PDF transformation engine. Once loaded:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Your PDF file is read directly from your local hard drive or smartphone storage into an in-memory <code>Uint8Array</code> buffer using the HTML5 File API.</li>
      <li>The QPDF WebAssembly module executes locally within your browser's sandboxed V8 execution engine, utilizing your device's CPU and RAM.</li>
      <li>If a User Password is provided, the cryptographic AES or RC4 decryption takes place entirely offline in your browser's memory.</li>
      <li>The unencrypted document is serialized and converted into a local <code>blob:</code> URL for download. <strong>Not a single byte of your document or password ever touches our servers.</strong></li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      You can verify this yourself: once this webpage is loaded, you can completely turn off your Wi-Fi, disconnect your mobile data, or switch your device into Airplane mode. The unlocker will continue to process and decrypt your PDF files flawlessly offline.
    </p>

    <h3>Step-by-Step: How to Unlock Any Protected PDF File in Seconds</h3>
    <div style="margin: 32px 0;">
