      <div class="faq-accordion-item">
        <h4 class="faq-accordion-q">2. Why do visa centers and government portals reject password-protected PDF files?</h4>
        <p class="faq-accordion-a">
          Visa processing agencies (such as VFS Global, BLS International, and foreign consulates) and government recruitment portals use automated document indexing pipelines. When an applicant submits a password-protected PDF, automated OCR parsers and batch document collators cannot open or render the file without human intervention. Because case officers process thousands of applications daily, password-locked documents are instantly rejected for failing basic submission guidelines. Always unlock your bank statements, payslips, and tax returns before submitting them for visa or job applications.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-accordion-item">
        <h4 class="faq-accordion-q">3. Why does my PDF display "(SECURED)" in the title bar even though it opened without a password?</h4>
        <p class="faq-accordion-a">
          When a PDF displays "(SECURED)" without prompting you for a password to open, the file is protected with an "Owner Password" (also known as a permissions password). The author has restricted administrative permissions such as high-resolution printing, copying text, extracting pages, or filling forms. In this state, the underlying text and vectors are not encrypted with a user key. Our offline unlocker instantly identifies owner restrictions, clears the permission bitmask, and produces an unrestricted PDF without asking you to type any password.
