          Yes, it is 100% safe. Unlike traditional online PDF tools that upload your confidential files to remote web servers, our tool runs an offline WebAssembly (Wasm) port of QPDF directly inside your browser. Your PDF document and the password you type remain exclusively in your computer or phone's local memory (RAM). No data is transmitted across the internet, and no copies are stored on any server. You can even disconnect your internet connection before unlocking your file to verify complete privacy.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-accordion-item">
        <h4 class="faq-accordion-q">5. Does unlocking a PDF alter its formatting, fonts, or digital signatures?</h4>
        <p class="faq-accordion-a">
          Decryption with our tool is mathematically lossless. All fonts, vector layouts, high-resolution graphics, and metadata remain identical to the original file because the tool directly strips the /Encrypt security dictionary without rasterizing or flattening the pages into low-resolution images. Note that if the original document contained a cryptographic digital signature (PKCS#7 / PAdES), modifying any part of the PDF structure—including stripping encryption—will invalidate the digital signature certificate seal, though all visual signature stamps and text remain intact.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
